PuTTYgen for RISC OS

PuTTYgen makes the keys that let you log in to a computer with SSH without typing a
password: a key pair, which is a private key (which stays with you, locked with a
passphrase) and a public key (which you put on the computer you log in to).  This is a
RISC OS port of PuTTYgen from PuTTY 0.85 by Simon Tatham and others.

Starting
--------
Double-click !PuTTYgen to put its icon on the icon bar and open its window.  (PuTTY's
icon bar menu has Key generator..., which does the same.)  Click on the icon to open the
window again.  Menu on the icon gives Info, Help, Key generator... and Quit.  You can drop a
key file on the icon to load it.

Making a key
------------
1. Choose the kind of key.  RSA works everywhere; Ed25519 (EdDSA, 255 bits) is short and fast but
   needs a reasonably recent server; ECDSA is in between.  DSA is old and best avoided.
2. Choose the size: for RSA 2048 is fine (3072 or 4096 if you want more margin), for ECDSA 256,
   384 or 521, for EdDSA 255 (Ed25519) or 448 (Ed448).
3. Click Generate.  The bar shows how far it has got; Cancel stops it.  A big RSA key can take a
   while on a slow computer - a minute or more for 4096 bits on an old machine.
4. Type a comment if you like (a note to tell this key from others), and a passphrase twice.
   The passphrase locks the private key; without one anyone who gets the file can use it.
5. Click Save private key and save the .ppk file somewhere safe: drag the file icon to a
   directory display, or type the whole pathname and click Save.
6. Click Copy public key to put the public key on the clipboard, and paste it into the file
   ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on the computer you want to log in to - or click Save public key
   to save it as a file.

To use the key in PuTTY, give its .ppk file in the Key file box of the Connect box (or drag
the file onto that box).

Randomness
----------
A key is only as good as the random numbers it is made from.  PuTTYgen takes them from the
CryptRandom module, which PuTTY loads from !System if it is there (get it with !PackMan).
Without it PuTTYgen asks you to move the mouse over its window until the bar has filled, and
uses the movements, as PuTTYgen does on other systems.

Loading a key
-------------
Drop a key file on the window (or click Load... and type its pathname) to look at it again,
to change its comment or passphrase and save it, or to convert it.  PuTTY's own .ppk files
load, and so do the keys made by OpenSSH (ssh-keygen) and ssh.com.  If the key is locked,
PuTTYgen asks for its passphrase.

The window's menu (click Menu anywhere in it)
---------------------------------------------
  Load key...          the same as the Load button
  Save public key      the public key as a file, in OpenSSH's one-line format
  Save private key     the private key as a PuTTY .ppk file
  Copy public key      the public key on the clipboard
  Export               save the private key in another program's format:
                         OpenSSH key           (for OpenSSH's ssh and ssh-agent)
                         OpenSSH (new format)  (always the newer OpenSSH file format)
                         ssh.com key
                         Public key (RFC 4716) the public key in the format ssh.com
                                               servers and ssh-keygen -i read
  Fingerprint          show the fingerprint as SHA256 (the default) or MD5

The fingerprint is a short summary of the public key: if two copies show the same one, they
are the same key.

Filetypes
---------
Key files are saved as Text (&FFF).  Take care of your private key: anyone who has the file
and the passphrase (or the file alone, if you gave no passphrase) can log in as you.

